-- title:
--   Inertia at 2 acts by cyclotomic exponent on Eisenstein torsion displacements
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ and $q$ be primes with $q = 2$ and $q \neq p$, and let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $q$ in the sense that the image of $q$ is a nonunit of $A$. The assertion is: for every natural number $m$ and every function $n$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}) = (\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \simeq_{\mathbb{Q}} \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $\mathbb{N}$ satisfying $\sigma\zeta = \zeta^{n(\sigma)}$ for all $\sigma$ and all $\zeta \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta^{q^m} = 1$, and for all $\sigma, \tau$ in `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` (the image in the Galois group of the inertia subgroup of $A$ inside its decomposition subgroup) and every $x$ in `eisensteinTorsionBar p q m`, one has $$\tau \cdot (\sigma \cdot x - x) = n(\tau) \cdot (\sigma \cdot x - x)$$ in `JZero p`, the right-hand side being the $\mathbb{N}$-multiple. Here `JZero p` is the group of degree-zero divisor classes $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $p$, with its Galois action, and `eisensteinTorsionBar p q m` is the subgroup annihilated by the $m$-th power of the Eisenstein ideal `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p q` — the preimage under `eisensteinEval p` of the ideal generated by $q$ in $\mathbb{Z}$ — inside the Hecke algebra `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb{Z}[T_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ acting through `heckeModuleBar p`.
--
--   This is the case $q = 2$ of Mazur's inertia computation on the Eisenstein $\mathfrak{P}^m$-torsion of $J_0(p)$: inertia displacements $\sigma x - x$ lie in a part of the torsion on which inertia acts through the cyclotomic exponent. It feeds the prime-independent statement [`ModularCurve.eisensteinTorsionBar_inertia_smul_sub_eq_nat_smul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eisensteinTorsionBar_inertia_smul_sub_eq_nat_smul), the $2$-adic case being separated because the classification of finite flat group schemes used for odd $q$ does not apply.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_eisensteinTorsionBar_inertia_smul_sub_eq_nat_smul_two.lean

